# Orchestrator config

Copy this file to `.agent-workspace/config.md` and edit it for your project.
`config.md` is gitignored; this `.example` is the committed template.

## Target repos

Every repo the orchestrator may work on. Add one block per repo. The number of
repos is just the length of this list — one repo or N repos is the same code
path to the pipeline.

`test` is optional: a bounded command that runs the repo's test suite and exits
non-zero on failure (e.g. `npm test`, `pytest`). When present, the implementer
runs it to verify before opening/updating a PR, and the reviewer may run it to
confirm the suite is green. Omit it if the repo has no test suite.

```yaml
repos:
  - slug: example-api
    path: /absolute/path/to/example-api
    test: npm test
  - slug: example-web
    path: /absolute/path/to/example-web
    test: npm test
```

## Tracker

Where tasks come from. One of `github`, `gitlab`, or `none`.

- `github` / `gitlab`: the orchestrator reads the task from an issue/ticket.
- `none`: the orchestrator reads the task from
  `.agent-workspace/feature-request.md`.

```yaml
tracker: none
```

## Conventions

`commit_convention` controls how commit messages are formatted:

- `conventional` (default): [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org).
  Maps cleanly onto Tidy First — the structural commit uses `refactor:`, the
  behavioral commit uses `feat:` / `fix:` (or `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:` as
  appropriate). Enables automated changelogs and semver.
- `default`: free-form clear messages, structural commit then behavioral commit.

Either way the structural/behavioral split is enforced; the convention only
changes the message format, not the commit discipline.

`base_branch` is the ref every feature branch is cut from (passed as the base
to `worktree-create.sh`). Set it to the repo's integration branch (e.g. `main`
or `master`). If you leave it unset, worktrees branch from whatever the repo has
checked out at run time — which is rarely what you want, so set it explicitly.

`max_fix_cycles` caps the review→fix loop per sub-task (default `3`). When the
cap is hit without a `CLEAN` verdict, the sub-task is labeled `ready-for-human`
and handed off. The **existence** of a finite cap with a human escape hatch is a
design invariant; only the number is configurable. Lower it (e.g. `2`) to
escalate to a human sooner, or raise it to be more patient — but a very high
value defeats the purpose (agents loop instead of asking a human).

```yaml
branch_convention: "feat/<task>-<repo-slug>"
base_branch: main
commit_convention: conventional
max_fix_cycles: 3
```
